    "source": "Source text and provenance checked 28 October 2026. Passage source checked 29 October 2026. https://sr.wikisource.org/sr-el/Емина",
    "id": "bosnia-and-herzegovina",
    "contextNote": "Aleksa Šantić wrote in Mostar soon after Austria-Hungary took Bosnia over from the Ottoman Empire. Emina, about a Muslim girl in a garden, became a beloved sevdalinka, the region's traditional love song, sung by people of every community in a city later divided by war.",
    "formGroup": "Poem",
    "display": {
      "title": "Emina",
      "english": "",
      "creator": "Aleksa Šantić",
      "language": "Bosnian / Serbian"
    },
    "passage": {
      "original": "Sinoć, kad se vratih iz topla hamama,\nProđoh pokraj bašte staroga imama;\nKad tamo, u bašti, u hladu jasmina,\nS ibrikom u ruci stajaše Emina.\nJa kakva je, pusta! Tako mi imana,\nStid je ne bi bilo da je kod sultana!\nPa još kad se šeće i plećima kreće...\n- Ni hodžin mi zapis više pomoć neće!...\nJa joj nazvah selam. Al' moga mi dina,\nNe šće ni da čuje lijepa Emina,\nNo u srebren ibrik zahitila vode\nPa po bašti đule zalivati ode;\nS grana vjetar duhnu pa niz pleći puste\nRasplete joj one pletenice guste,",
      "english": "Last night, returning from the warm bathhouse,\nI passed beside the old imam’s garden;\nand there, in the garden, in the jasmine’s shade,\nstood Emina, a water-jug in her hand.\nOh, what a beauty! I swear by my faith,\nshe would have no cause for shame before the sultan!\nAnd when she walks and sways her shoulders—\nnot even the hodja’s charm can help me now!\nI greeted her with salaam. But, by my faith,\nlovely Emina would not even listen;\nshe filled her silver jug with water\nand went through the garden watering the roses.\nA breeze blew from the branches and down her shoulders\nunwound those thick braids of hers.",
      "status": "draft"
    },
