    "contextNote": "Hélène Swarth was one of the finest Dutch poets of the 1880s and 1890s, known for intense poems of love and loss. Born in Amsterdam, she grew up in Brussels writing in French, and only later turned to Dutch.",
    "formGroup": "Poem",
    "display": {
      "title": "Meibloesems, III — Breek blanke meien van den Levensboom",
      "english": "May Blossoms, III — Break white May branches from the Tree of Life",
      "creator": "Hélène Swarth",
      "language": "Dutch"
    },
    "passage": {
      "original": "Breek blanke meien van den Levensboom!\nNu is de Mei gekomen, ook voor mij.\nAls witte vlinders fladdren ze ons voorbij,\nDe bloesemblaadren van mijn lentedroom.\nZie, dit is weelde: wandlen zij aan zij\nIn 't bloeiend Eden onzer liefde, loom\nEn stil van 't groot geluk, met teedren schroom\nOntvangen en gedragen, bang en blij.\nNu laat mij leunen aan uw borst, mijn lief,\nEn leg uw streelhand op mijn lokken zacht\nEn zeg of 't u wel heugt, die eerste brief,\nDie eerste kus en o die winternacht,\nToen Liefde mij in 't hart sloop als een dief,\nNu scepter-zwaaiende in vorstinnepracht.",
      "english": "Break white May blossom from the Tree of Life!\nNow May has come, for me as well.\nLike white butterflies they flutter past us,\nthe blossom petals of my spring dream.\nSee, this is bliss: to wander side by side\nin the blossoming Eden of our love, languid\nand quiet with great happiness, with tender shyness\nreceived and carried, fearful and glad.\nNow let me lean against your breast, my love,\nand lay your stroking hand softly on my hair,\nand tell me if you remember it, that first letter,\nthat first kiss, and oh, that winter night\nwhen Love crept into my heart like a thief,\nand now waves his sceptre in the splendour of a queen.",
      "status": "draft"
    },
